    <paragraph>Determine Whether a Function is One-to-One</paragraph>
    <paragraph>In the following exercises, determine if the set of ordered pairs represents a function and if so, is the function one-to-one.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="13" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left\{ {\left( {-3,9} \right),\left( {-2,4} \right),\left( {-1,1} \right),\left( {0,0} \right)} \right.$$ , $$\left. \left( {1,1} \right),\left( {2,4} \right),\left( {3,9} \right) \right\}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Function; not one-to-one</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="14" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$\left\{ {\left( {9,-3} \right),\left( {4,-2} \right),\left( {1,-1} \right),\left( {0,0} \right)} \right.$$ , $$\left. \left( {1,1} \right),\left( {4,2} \right),\left( {9,3} \right) \right\}$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="15" has_solution="true">
